  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 PM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nLooking at the long-term forecast into next week, the complexity \nremains along with uncertainty between model runs. There will be two \ninteracting low pressure systems, one coming down from the Pacific \nNorthwest and another moving onto the CA coast. \n\nSunday-Monday...\n\nSunday and Monday will remain warm, with dry conditions persisting \nover the eastern plains. Highs will remain in the 70s-80s across the \nplains, with 60s over the valleys. Over the higher terrain, moisture \nwill continue to increase, with widespread precipitation over the \nSan Juans specifically. Activity looks to lessen slightly on Monday, \nwith a brief lull just ahead of the incoming systems, though \nisolated to scattered POPs remain over the Continental Divide, again \nwith the best coverage over the San Juans and adjacent areas. As of \nnow, most of, if not all of the precip should fall as rain, with \nsnow levels remaining well above 12 kft. \n\nTuesday and Onwards...\n\nThe rest of the long-term forecast becomes much more complex, as \nmodels continuously struggle to deal with the phasing of the \nmultiple interacting lows, as well as the speed and amplitude of the \nresulting trough as it pushes into our region. Initial cold front is \nexpected to move onto the plains early on Tuesday, giving us much \ncooler temperatures out east and some additional low-level moisture. \nAround this time, the influx of cooler air will allow for a switch \nfrom rain to snow over our higher mountains, so Tuesday will be our \nfirm starting point for tracking snow accumulation in the models. \n\nModels generally have the low hitting the Four Corners on Wednesday, \nthough the north-south placement is still very much in question. The \nexact path the low takes will have sizable impacts on how cold we \nget and what kinds of impacts and weather we will see. Temperatures \nwill continue to cool as precip chances increase across the region. \nSnowfall will continue to hit the mountains while the lower \nelevations still solely receive rain. Current long-range guidance \nstill keeps the majority of the moisture over the mountains, but a \nfew showers will still be possible over the eastern plains as well.\n\nStill plenty of uncertainty between the models at this point, and \nplenty of time for the details to become more clear. But the trends \nare heavily leaning towards a cooler and unsettled pattern, \nparticularly for the latter half of next week.",
